WEBVTT

1
00:00:02.700 --> 00:00:07.898
The capstone is one final project that uses every part of the course together.

2
00:00:07.898 --> 00:00:11.631
One small code change travels from your laptop to users.

3
00:00:11.833 --> 00:00:13.476
Fourteen stages, in order.

4
00:00:13.476 --> 00:00:17.265
And at every stage, you can prove that the change got there.

5
00:00:17.467 --> 00:00:19.053
So when is a stage done?

6
00:00:19.053 --> 00:00:20.574
Not when it looks fine.

7
00:00:20.574 --> 00:00:22.756
When its acceptance check passes.

8
00:00:23.367 --> 00:00:25.189
Take stage 7, GitOps.

9
00:00:25.189 --> 00:00:28.052
A weak check says: Argo CD works.

10
00:00:28.267 --> 00:00:34.887
A strong check says: after a merge, with no kubectl from you, this command reports Synced and Healthy.

11
00:00:35.100 --> 00:00:37.325
A good check has three properties.

12
00:00:37.325 --> 00:00:41.643
It is observable: it looks at the running system, not your memory.

13
00:00:41.643 --> 00:00:44.980
It is binary: anyone who runs it gets PASS or FAIL.

14
00:00:44.980 --> 00:00:47.073
And it is runnable: one command.

15
00:00:47.300 --> 00:00:49.629
The weak version cannot clearly fail.

16
00:00:49.629 --> 00:00:51.644
So passing it tells you nothing.

17
00:00:52.367 --> 00:00:53.891
Here is the pattern.

18
00:00:53.891 --> 00:00:56.635
Ask the running app for its version.

19
00:00:56.635 --> 00:00:58.845
If it says 1.2.0, print PASS.

20
00:00:58.845 --> 00:01:00.521
Otherwise, print FAIL.

21
00:01:00.733 --> 00:01:05.263
Every line of the lab's capstone-check.sh follows this pattern.

22
00:01:05.967 --> 00:01:10.034
The script turns 11 of the 14 stages into checks like that.

23
00:01:10.034 --> 00:01:12.516
Each line prints PASS, FAIL or SKIP.

24
00:01:12.516 --> 00:01:15.480
SKIP means a tool or an address is missing.

25
00:01:15.700 --> 00:01:19.599
It ends with a count, and exits with 1 if anything failed.

26
00:01:19.599 --> 00:01:22.220
So the same file could run in CI later.

27
00:01:22.433 --> 00:01:25.235
Run it early, and almost everything fails.

28
00:01:25.235 --> 00:01:26.236
That is normal.

29
00:01:26.236 --> 00:01:29.237
Read the output as your to-do list, in order.

30
00:01:29.467 --> 00:01:32.007
Finish a stage, and run the script again.

31
00:01:32.007 --> 00:01:34.113
When its line turns green, commit.

32
00:01:34.113 --> 00:01:37.273
Now you can always go back to the last green point.

33
00:01:37.500 --> 00:01:38.899
Then the next stage.

34
00:01:38.899 --> 00:01:41.417
GitOps passes, and you commit again.

35
00:01:42.033 --> 00:01:43.972
Now you start the next stage.

36
00:01:43.972 --> 00:01:49.321
Along the way, a new port-forward grabs port 8081, where the app used to answer.

37
00:01:49.533 --> 00:01:51.235
You were working on metrics.

38
00:01:51.235 --> 00:01:56.885
But the line that turns red is an old one: the app check, from a stage you finished long ago.

39
00:01:57.100 --> 00:02:00.140
That old red line points you straight at the cause.

40
00:02:00.140 --> 00:02:02.703
You fix the port, and it turns green again.

41
00:02:02.933 --> 00:02:07.528
So run it after every stage, and read every line, not only the newest one.

42
00:02:07.528 --> 00:02:12.184
You run the whole test suite for the same reason, not only the newest test.

43
00:02:12.800 --> 00:02:14.181
Compare the other way.

44
00:02:14.181 --> 00:02:17.509
Build all fourteen stages first, and test at the end.

45
00:02:17.509 --> 00:02:22.783
Now five things are broken at once, and you cannot tell which one causes the others.

46
00:02:23.800 --> 00:02:25.080
One stage at a time.

47
00:02:25.080 --> 00:02:27.511
A stage is done when its check passes.

48
00:02:27.511 --> 00:02:31.927
Run all the checks after every stage, and commit at each green point.
